text:
The diff changes the year constant from `var y = d * 365.25` (milliseconds in 365.25 days = 31,557,600,000 ms) to `var y = d / 365.25` (≈236,550.31 ms, about 3.9 minutes). This constant is used when parsing year strings, so `ms('1y')`, `ms('1yr')`, `ms('1year')`, etc. now return a value ~133,000× too small. (Formatting is unaffected in this version since `fmtShort`/`fmtLong` never emit years, but parsing is plainly broken.)

```json
{
  "defect": true,
  "testFile": {
    "path": "proof-test.js",
    "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var ms = require('./');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('fails on the patched build', function () {
    // One year must be 365.25 days of milliseconds, not 1/365.25 of a day.
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1y'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1yr'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1year'), 31557600000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('100y'), 3155760000000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('-1y'), -31557600000);
  });
});
"
  },
  "note": "The year constant was inverted: y = d / 365.25 is ~236550.31 ms (about 3.9 minutes) instead of d * 365.25 = 31557600000 ms (365.25 days), so every year string parses to a wildly wrong value (ms('1y') returns ~236550.31 instead of 31557600000). Formatting never emits years in 2.1.3, so only year parsing is observably broken."
}
```

